            "body": "## Code Review\n\nThis pull request addresses issue #37419 by ensuring that the `redundancy_buffer_memory` is correctly subtracted from the available memory during KV cache allocation. This change aims to prevent runtime OOM errors by providing a safety margin for caching allocator fragmentation and memory profiling inaccuracy. The buffer subtraction is moved into `determine_available_memory()` for consistency, and the value is stored on `self` for reuse. I have identified one high severity issue related to the hardcoded buffer size.",
            "comments": {
              "nodes": [
                {
                  "author": {
                    "__typename": "Bot",
                    "login": "gemini-code-assist"
                  },
                  "body": "![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)\n\nThe redundancy buffer size is hardcoded. This makes it difficult to adjust the buffer size without modifying the code. It would be better to make this configurable via a command-line argument or a configuration file.\n\nIf the buffer is too small, it might not be sufficient to prevent OOM errors. If it's too large, it could lead to inefficient memory utilization. Making it configurable allows users to tune the buffer size based on their specific needs and hardware constraints.\n\n```python\n        # Leave a configurable buffer for caching allocator fragmentation\n        # and memory profiling inaccuracy to reduce risk of OOM at runtime.\n        self.redundancy_buffer_memory = self.vllm_config.cache_config.redundancy_buffer_size\n```",
                  "createdAt": "2026-03-18T14:10:59Z",
                  "databaseId": 2953723613,
                  "diffHunk": "@@ -430,10 +430,14 @@ def determine_available_memory(self) -> int:\n             \"To fix this, ensure consistent GPU memory allocation or \"\n             \"isolate vLLM in its own container.\"\n         )\n+        # Leave a small buffer (150 MiB) for caching allocator fragmentation\n+        # and memory profiling inaccuracy to reduce risk of OOM at runtime.\n+        self.redundancy_buffer_memory = 150 * (1 << 20)",
                  "line": 463,
